1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
It was the technology that fueled the Third Reich's war machine.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
State-of-the-art weaponry designed for world domination,

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:13,000
combined with some of the greatest inventions of the 20th century.

4
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:16,000
Their research led to the electron microscope,

5
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:20,000
audio tape, and even a link between smoking and cancer.

6
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:24,000
But these discoveries sometimes came at great human cost.

7
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:28,000
High Tech Hitler, next on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
The rise of Adolf Hitler to power was based on the ideology of an all-powerful, all-conquering

9
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:48,318
master race.

10
00:00:48,319 --> 00:00:53,240
It followed that anything that undermined the health of the Aryan race had

11
00:00:53,241 --> 00:00:54,372
to be eradicated.

12
00:00:54,373 --> 00:00:59,300
It was not surprising then that it was German doctors who first discovered

13
00:00:59,301 --> 00:01:02,036
the link between smoking and cancer.

14
00:01:02,037 --> 00:01:05,760
For the Nazis, the abuse of tobacco fitted into a

15
00:01:05,761 --> 00:01:10,860
broader ideology of eugenics and racial purity.

16
00:01:10,861 --> 00:01:19,120
The Nazis had this view that your body belonged to the Fuhrer, your body belonged to the nation.

17
00:01:19,121 --> 00:01:24,660
You were not supposed to abuse your body because you were really hurting state property when

18
00:01:24,661 --> 00:01:27,603
you smoked or when you drank or when you used drugs.

19
00:01:27,604 --> 00:01:29,640
And as a result, there was this view

20
00:01:29,641 --> 00:01:33,900
that anything you did that was hurting your body was hurting the state.

21
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:36,756
Hitler's own health was fragile.

22
00:01:36,757 --> 00:01:40,260
He consumed an array of medicines and potions for his

23
00:01:40,261 --> 00:01:42,198
stomach, bowels, and heart.

24
00:01:42,199 --> 00:01:46,360
But he was also a teetotaler and a vegetarian, a lifestyle

25
00:01:46,361 --> 00:01:48,540
he thought appropriate for the master race.

26
00:01:48,541 --> 00:01:59,180
you had the aryan man and they often looked like these weak statues they also wanted to eat right

27
00:01:59,181 --> 00:02:05,060
you know fiber and fruits and vegetables everything we say these days about eating healthy you had

28
00:02:05,061 --> 00:02:09,500
these movements in other countries as well but it wasn't part of state ideology and when you

29
00:02:09,501 --> 00:02:14,860
have a totalitarian centralized government it can become a part of state policy

30
00:02:14,861 --> 00:02:23,860
The ideology of the master race demanded super fit young men and athletic girls.

31
00:02:23,861 --> 00:02:30,860
Propaganda films promoted healthy living and outdoor activities in the dramatic landscape of the fatherland.

32
00:02:30,861 --> 00:02:39,860
But a healthy nation also meant a broad assault on what German scientists of the time identified as threats to public health.

33
00:02:39,861 --> 00:02:46,620
I think of the Nazi period as this quest for a sanitary utopia,

34
00:02:47,380 --> 00:02:48,860
an effort to clean up the air.

35
00:02:49,380 --> 00:02:50,460
They wanted clean water.

36
00:02:51,220 --> 00:02:53,260
They actually even wanted a healthy workplace.

37
00:02:53,261 --> 00:02:59,960
You find the first good studies on the tobacco causation of cancer in the Nazi period,

38
00:03:00,040 --> 00:03:03,720
the first studies of asbestos causing lung cancer in the Nazi period,

39
00:03:04,020 --> 00:03:08,680
the first studies even of indoor radon causing lung cancer,

40
00:03:08,681 --> 00:03:12,360
And many other carcinogens are first identified in the Nazi period.

41
00:03:13,820 --> 00:03:20,080
So it was in Germany in the 1930s that the first serious and systematic attempt was made

42
00:03:20,081 --> 00:03:22,900
to establish a link between cancer and smoking.

43
00:03:23,360 --> 00:03:30,000
What you have in the Nazi period is animal experiments that were large-scale using many animals.

44
00:03:30,240 --> 00:03:33,920
You have the development of case-control epidemiological methods,

45
00:03:33,921 --> 00:03:37,680
that is, methods where you would compare one population that smoked

46
00:03:37,681 --> 00:03:41,280
with a very carefully balanced, similar population,

47
00:03:41,440 --> 00:03:45,980
same age, same race, same health condition, who didn't smoke.

48
00:03:46,060 --> 00:03:49,600
And then you would see which had the higher rate of lung cancer,

49
00:03:49,660 --> 00:03:52,960
and they found that there was a significantly higher lung cancer rate

50
00:03:52,961 --> 00:03:54,500
among the smoking population.

51
00:03:56,020 --> 00:03:59,780
Once the link had been established between cigarette smoking and cancer,

52
00:03:59,781 --> 00:04:04,680
it was clear that every effort had to be made to protect the master race from its effects.

53
00:04:07,580 --> 00:04:10,300
Safeguarding the purity of the stock was critical.

54
00:04:12,220 --> 00:04:16,300
Anything that might endanger the health of the Aryan child was forbidden.

55
00:04:17,720 --> 00:04:21,920
They thought that especially German women, by smoking,

56
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:24,400
would be reducing the fitness of their offspring.

57
00:04:24,401 --> 00:04:31,400
This was viewed as something that was actually a crime against the German fruit of the womb, you might say.

58
00:04:31,401 --> 00:04:37,400
And that by smoking, you were actually damaging the future of the German race.

59
00:04:37,401 --> 00:04:41,400
Well, that certainly fit in with the eugenic policy,

60
00:04:41,401 --> 00:04:46,400
that if you're going to breed a better man, you want them to be healthy.

61
00:04:46,401 --> 00:04:53,400
If smoking is bad for the organism and causes cancer, we don't want to perpetuate that in our race.

62
00:04:54,400 --> 00:04:57,655
Tobacco was attacked as an epidemic, a plague.

63
00:04:57,656 --> 00:05:01,760
Along with alcohol, it was a disease of liberal decadence.

64
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:07,180
Smoking was discouraged in the armed forces and banned in Nazi party offices.

65
00:05:08,340 --> 00:05:13,240
By 1943, it was illegal for under 18-year-olds to smoke in public.

66
00:05:14,420 --> 00:05:17,780
And a year later, smoking was outlawed on city trains and buses.

67
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:24,260
Hitler banned anyone from smoking in his presence, though he had been a smoker himself in his youth.

68
00:05:24,400 --> 00:05:29,860
He even attributed the success of the National Socialist Party to his decision to give it up.

69
00:05:30,480 --> 00:05:33,280
He credits the triumph of fascism to his giving up smoking.

70
00:05:33,440 --> 00:05:36,740
He apparently smoked until around World War I,

71
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:41,180
where he says he threw his cigarettes into the Danube and never looked back.

72
00:05:41,300 --> 00:05:44,240
And he says if he hadn't given up smoking,

73
00:05:44,360 --> 00:05:47,500
he wouldn't have been able to organize the Nazi movement in Germany.

74
00:05:47,640 --> 00:05:50,720
So this was something very close to his heart.

75
00:05:51,520 --> 00:05:53,420
What's more, on this issue,

76
00:05:54,020 --> 00:05:57,220
Hitler was convinced he had the edge over the Allied leaders.

77
00:05:58,060 --> 00:06:00,720
One of the interesting things about the politics of this time

78
00:06:00,721 --> 00:06:04,360
is that all three of the fascist leaders,

79
00:06:04,500 --> 00:06:06,160
Mussolini, Franco, and Hitler,

80
00:06:06,700 --> 00:06:08,940
were at least non-smoking,

81
00:06:09,480 --> 00:06:11,540
whereas all three of the Allied leaders,

82
00:06:11,680 --> 00:06:14,380
Churchill, Roosevelt, and Stalin, were very heavy smokers.

83
00:06:14,381 --> 00:06:24,040
The American people, through their elected representatives in Congress, have determined

84
00:06:24,041 --> 00:06:26,680
to wage unremitting warfare against cancer.

85
00:06:27,540 --> 00:06:31,020
This is an American public information film made in 1940.

86
00:06:31,940 --> 00:06:34,760
It shows clearly that cancer was a public health issue.

87
00:06:35,380 --> 00:06:37,720
It gives advice about how to avoid the disease.

88
00:06:38,760 --> 00:06:42,420
Chronic irritation often influences the appearance of cancer.

89
00:06:42,421 --> 00:06:45,200
the chafing of a jagged tooth.

90
00:06:46,080 --> 00:06:49,860
But it makes no mention of smoking being a cause of cancer.

91
00:06:50,820 --> 00:06:55,880
It wasn't until 1950 that Allied scientists caught up with their German counterparts,

92
00:06:56,500 --> 00:07:00,300
and much later, before positive action was taken to deter smoking.

93
00:07:01,580 --> 00:07:04,620
The discovery of the link between smoking and cancer

94
00:07:04,621 --> 00:07:09,220
was an example of good science being usurped by the Nazis for their own ends.

95
00:07:09,221 --> 00:07:15,560
It gave ample ammunition for Nazi propagandists, spreading the ideology of the master race.

96
00:07:17,660 --> 00:07:23,460
And it was not long before Hitler's propaganda machine was exploiting a new technology

97
00:07:23,461 --> 00:07:28,240
that could spread the fascist message more effectively than ever before.

98
00:07:32,400 --> 00:07:38,600
In an effort to discourage smoking in the military, cigarette rations were limited to six per soldier per day.

99
00:07:39,260 --> 00:07:43,420
Non-smokers were offered alternative rations, such as chocolate or extra food.

100
00:07:44,180 --> 00:07:45,920
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

101
00:07:47,280 --> 00:07:51,240
We now return to High Tech Hitler on Modern Marvels.

102
00:07:54,040 --> 00:07:59,280
Scores of microphones and cameras were used to photograph Hitler and record his voice

103
00:07:59,281 --> 00:08:05,160
so that all of Germany and others throughout the world could receive his message of hate and hypocrisy.

104
00:08:05,161 --> 00:08:13,980
The rise of the Nazi party in the early 1930s depended in part on terror, but also on the

105
00:08:13,981 --> 00:08:19,120
ruthless use of propaganda.

106
00:08:19,121 --> 00:08:24,940
Hitler's cold-blooded and calculating propaganda minister was Joseph Goebbels.

107
00:08:24,941 --> 00:08:31,040
He had masterminded the presentation of Hitler's anti-Semitic message using all available technology

108
00:08:31,041 --> 00:08:35,040
at his disposal.

109
00:08:35,041 --> 00:08:40,100
But soon there would be a new invention that could transform Goebbels' propaganda machine

110
00:08:40,101 --> 00:08:44,560
and make it even easier for the Fuhrer to be heard throughout the fatherland.

111
00:08:44,561 --> 00:08:49,500
This new and revolutionary technology owed something to an obscure Austrian engineer

112
00:08:49,501 --> 00:08:54,220
living in Germany in the years after the First World War.

113
00:08:54,221 --> 00:08:57,140
His name was Fritz Floimer.

114
00:08:57,141 --> 00:09:02,940
In 1920s Germany, Floimer had the bright idea of coating paper with iron powder to make

115
00:09:02,941 --> 00:09:05,168
a cheap magnetic audio tape.

116
00:09:05,169 --> 00:09:10,100
This paper tape was fragile, but it would replace the existing

117
00:09:10,101 --> 00:09:16,060
expensive magnetic solid steel tape and could be edited with a pair of scissors rather than

118
00:09:16,061 --> 00:09:18,820
a soldering iron.

119
00:09:18,821 --> 00:09:21,380
Fleimer knew he was onto something.

120
00:09:21,381 --> 00:09:28,100
Fritz Fleimer took his invention to Berlin, to AEG, which was one of the leading electronics

121
00:09:28,101 --> 00:09:34,980
companies in those days and they bought the patents and so on from flymer and set up in 1932

122
00:09:34,981 --> 00:09:43,940
a team to begin work on das magnate phone the magnetic phonograph aeg teamed up with the german

123
00:09:43,941 --> 00:09:50,900
chemical giant ig farbin to produce the world's first magnetic tape recorder it would revolutionize

124
00:09:50,901 --> 00:09:57,460
sound recording but when aeg demonstrated their new invention at the berlin radio fair in 1935

125
00:09:58,100 --> 00:10:00,700
it was not quite the sensation they hoped for.

126
00:10:01,120 --> 00:10:02,800
The radio engineers looked at this and said,

127
00:10:02,860 --> 00:10:04,580
aha, this is it.

128
00:10:04,940 --> 00:10:07,180
Tape would be the ideal medium.

129
00:10:07,500 --> 00:10:09,620
We could record on it, we can snip it up,

130
00:10:09,840 --> 00:10:11,960
we can glue it or tape it back together

131
00:10:11,961 --> 00:10:14,200
and edit the shows to our heart's content.

132
00:10:14,520 --> 00:10:16,380
It would be a dream come true.

133
00:10:16,760 --> 00:10:19,800
One problem, the original AEG magnetophone

134
00:10:19,801 --> 00:10:24,280
shown at the 1935 radio fair sounded terrible.

135
00:10:24,281 --> 00:10:27,340
The fact was that the quality was good enough for speech,

136
00:10:27,341 --> 00:10:29,380
for use as a dictation machine.

137
00:10:30,700 --> 00:10:33,240
After all, the Nazis used the early magnetophone

138
00:10:33,241 --> 00:10:36,080
to listen into and record telephone conversations,

139
00:10:36,680 --> 00:10:39,580
but its commercial potential, as AEG recognized,

140
00:10:40,140 --> 00:10:41,200
was extremely limited.

141
00:10:42,380 --> 00:10:45,280
They instantly saw that there would be no business

142
00:10:45,281 --> 00:10:47,420
based on dictation purposes only.

143
00:10:47,421 --> 00:10:50,520
They had to have the possibility

144
00:10:50,521 --> 00:10:53,440
and the technical means to recording music.

145
00:10:53,580 --> 00:10:54,840
That would make the business.

146
00:10:54,841 --> 00:11:07,696
In 1936, as the Nazis consolidated their power in Germany, the world-famous conductor, Sir Thomas Beecham, took his orchestra to Berlin and made this recording on the

147
00:11:07,697 --> 00:11:08,780
new equipment.

148
00:11:09,980 --> 00:11:11,060
It was a disaster.

149
00:11:12,700 --> 00:11:14,465
It sounds truly terrible.

150
00:11:14,466 --> 00:11:24,140
In fact, Sir Thomas was so horrified by the quality of what he thought was tape that he reputedly refused to use it until, I think, 1951.

151
00:11:24,840 --> 00:11:34,040
Then, just as war was breaking out in 1939, came an accidental discovery that changed utterly the prospects of the magnetophone.

152
00:11:35,640 --> 00:11:45,460
A German radio engineer, Walter Weber, stumbled on a signal processing method called AC bias that enabled the magnetophone to record high fidelity.

153
00:11:46,160 --> 00:11:53,680
Weber one day was experimenting with circuits, advanced circuits that would try to get this horrible noise and distortion to a minimum

154
00:11:53,681 --> 00:11:55,640
and make the tape sound better.

155
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:58,980
And he discovered, quite by accident, AC bias.

156
00:11:59,060 --> 00:12:04,280
This oscillating tone you would superimpose over your recorded information

157
00:12:04,281 --> 00:12:06,520
that would make that recorded information stand out.

158
00:12:07,620 --> 00:12:11,360
A second test was carried out on the new improved magnetophone.

159
00:12:14,160 --> 00:12:18,840
It was the revolutionary breakthrough that German technicians had been searching for.

160
00:12:18,841 --> 00:12:25,840
This was the introduction of the high frequency bias magnetophone to the public.

161
00:12:25,841 --> 00:12:30,840
And it took place in a huge Berlin cinema and people were amazed.

162
00:12:30,841 --> 00:12:33,840
That was the moment. That was the moment.

163
00:12:33,841 --> 00:12:41,840
Everybody recognized instantly that's the best method for recording music and that will make a revolution.

164
00:12:41,841 --> 00:12:50,200
By the 1940s, as Germany launched its assault on the Soviet Union, the magnetophone became

165
00:12:50,201 --> 00:12:53,300
a must-have item for wealthy Germans.

166
00:12:53,301 --> 00:12:58,880
An American document at the time described how Hitler himself, at the height of the fighting,

167
00:12:58,881 --> 00:13:01,440
acquired his own machine.

168
00:13:01,441 --> 00:13:06,580
Hitler was first introduced to the magnetophone in his headquarters on the Russian front in

169
00:13:06,581 --> 00:13:12,780
1942 and immediately he ordered a plane to bring additional symphony recordings from

170
00:13:12,781 --> 00:13:13,780
Berlin.

171
00:13:13,781 --> 00:13:14,980
So Hitler was photographed.

172
00:13:14,981 --> 00:13:19,780
The Nazi propaganda machine knew when it was on to a good thing and exploited the new

173
00:13:19,781 --> 00:13:22,580
possibilities that the magnetophone offered.

174
00:13:22,581 --> 00:13:35,860
There were about six recordings of Adolf Hitler's speeches during the world war.

175
00:13:35,861 --> 00:13:40,400
Hitler didn't come to the studio, so the studio came to Adolf Hitler,

176
00:13:40,880 --> 00:13:45,720
and they used the transportable units of magnetophones to record these speeches.

177
00:13:48,600 --> 00:13:53,980
These recordings were edited and broadcasted to the public for propaganda news.

178
00:13:54,520 --> 00:14:00,980
Hitler sobbing, Hitler smiling, Hitler shouting, Hitler working his people into a frenzy.

179
00:14:00,981 --> 00:14:10,340
Hitler's propagandists discovered the magnetophone could be used to time shift the Fuhrer's speeches.

180
00:14:11,780 --> 00:14:17,500
With a sophisticated radio network inherited by the Nazis from the previous democratic regime,

181
00:14:18,360 --> 00:14:22,800
Hitler could record a speech on tape in one city for transmission in another.

182
00:14:23,520 --> 00:14:30,100
There were even suggestions that Hitler used the new technology to deceive his enemies as to where precisely he was.

183
00:14:30,101 --> 00:14:40,426
There's a persistent myth that still lives today that, you know, Hitler ordered the invention of the magnetophone tape recorder to fool the Allies as to his

184
00:14:40,427 --> 00:14:41,220
whereabouts.

185
00:14:41,700 --> 00:14:42,940
Well, that's nonsense.

186
00:14:43,720 --> 00:14:48,440
When high-fidelity tape recording came along, yes, they recorded Hitler.

187
00:14:48,640 --> 00:14:52,200
Yes, his speeches were tape-delayed and sent around.

188
00:14:52,201 --> 00:14:58,180
So, of course, anybody in the Allied command listening to monitoring German broadcasting would hear him pop up.

189
00:14:58,181 --> 00:15:00,780
gee, this is coming out of the Munich transmitter,

190
00:15:00,860 --> 00:15:02,120
and all of a sudden we're getting a speech,

191
00:15:02,200 --> 00:15:04,240
and he's making local references to Hamburg,

192
00:15:04,700 --> 00:15:06,160
and now he's in Berlin.

193
00:15:06,380 --> 00:15:07,340
How are they doing this?

194
00:15:07,560 --> 00:15:11,960
Well, the same way they always did, clear back to the 1920s,

195
00:15:12,260 --> 00:15:16,120
with an advanced, high-quality, live network

196
00:15:16,121 --> 00:15:19,700
with some sort of time-delay device, in this case, tape.

197
00:15:21,400 --> 00:15:23,420
As the war drew to a close,

198
00:15:23,421 --> 00:15:28,320
the plunder of Nazi science and secrets by the victorious allies began.

199
00:15:29,840 --> 00:15:33,080
U.S. Army Signals Corps Major Jack Mullen and his team

200
00:15:33,081 --> 00:15:36,320
were given the task of rooting out new German electronics.

201
00:15:38,300 --> 00:15:42,420
Mullen had been intrigued by the quality of late-night wartime German broadcasts.

202
00:15:44,060 --> 00:15:48,140
I thought I was pretty familiar with what all types of recording were,

203
00:15:48,320 --> 00:15:52,460
film and disc, and this sounded superb.

204
00:15:52,461 --> 00:15:56,500
and we thought probably Hitler must be having people work all night.

205
00:15:57,260 --> 00:16:01,640
Then, on a visit to a radio station a few weeks after the German surrender,

206
00:16:02,460 --> 00:16:05,500
Mullen heard an AC-bias hi-fi magnetophone.

207
00:16:05,760 --> 00:16:08,220
I'd never heard anything like that in my life before.

208
00:16:08,500 --> 00:16:10,160
I couldn't hear any background noise.

209
00:16:10,440 --> 00:16:13,740
Very clean, no distortion that I was able to hear.

210
00:16:14,640 --> 00:16:18,800
Mullen copied the blueprints, shipped two surplus machines back to America,

211
00:16:18,801 --> 00:16:23,760
and in May 1946 went public with his Americanized tape recorders.

212
00:16:24,240 --> 00:16:27,220
It changed the course of the broadcast and music industries.

213
00:16:27,740 --> 00:16:28,720
It was a sensation.

214
00:16:29,120 --> 00:16:35,820
May 16, 1946 is a breakthrough date in American tape recording technology.

215
00:16:36,980 --> 00:16:40,440
Where the blue of the night

216
00:16:40,441 --> 00:16:45,980
Bing Crosby was the first Hollywood star to use this new technology from Nazi Germany.

217
00:16:45,981 --> 00:16:49,920
Crosby found live radio a chore

218
00:16:49,921 --> 00:16:52,400
and when he heard about the amazing new machines

219
00:16:52,401 --> 00:16:56,100
he hired Mullen to record and edit his show for later broadcast

220
00:16:56,101 --> 00:16:57,720
it was a first

221
00:16:57,721 --> 00:17:00,840
the little piece of tape that I'm going to play on here

222
00:17:00,841 --> 00:17:04,880
was actually recorded in 1947

223
00:17:04,881 --> 00:17:08,980
and it still sounds as good as it ever did

224
00:17:08,981 --> 00:17:13,840
where the blue of the night

225
00:17:13,841 --> 00:17:18,580
meets the goal of the day.

226
00:17:19,740 --> 00:17:23,560
The first show, Bing's rating shot back up.

227
00:17:23,940 --> 00:17:29,140
The switchboard at ABC-NBC was flooded with calls from L.A. residents.

228
00:17:29,141 --> 00:17:31,560
Why didn't you tell us Bing was going to be live?

229
00:17:31,680 --> 00:17:33,060
We wanted tickets.

230
00:17:33,580 --> 00:17:37,360
So post-war, the magnetophone revolutionized sound recording.

231
00:17:37,960 --> 00:17:42,120
But for the Nazis, it was simply an existing technology they could exploit,

232
00:17:42,121 --> 00:17:45,200
a pattern for much of the science used by the Nazis.

233
00:17:46,080 --> 00:17:50,200
Indeed, some historians dismiss the traditional view of teams of researchers

234
00:17:50,201 --> 00:17:52,600
ruthlessly pursuing a Nazi science.

235
00:17:53,160 --> 00:17:55,160
It was often much more haphazard.

236
00:17:56,340 --> 00:17:59,020
And if you think of the popular image of the Nazi,

237
00:17:59,180 --> 00:18:05,380
you have the immaculately tailored officer and the professor in his crisp laboratory coat,

238
00:18:05,520 --> 00:18:08,140
and they're dealing with the highest level of technology,

239
00:18:08,240 --> 00:18:09,820
and they have some maniacal plan.

240
00:18:09,821 --> 00:18:14,820
And the maniacal plan idea is absolutely correct, but the rest of it is totally false.

241
00:18:15,820 --> 00:18:20,820
In fact, one of the most important scientific inventions of the 20th century

242
00:18:20,821 --> 00:18:26,820
was developed by German scientists, and the Nazi leadership didn't seem to care.

243
00:18:27,820 --> 00:18:34,820
Bing Crosby was so impressed by the magnetophone that he invested $50,000 of his own money into Ampex,

244
00:18:34,821 --> 00:18:38,820
the company which soon became the world leader in the development of tape recording.

245
00:18:38,821 --> 00:18:52,821
This is the common view of science and technology under the Nazis.

246
00:18:54,820 --> 00:19:01,520
The mentally ill and the victims of the concentration camps were used as guinea pigs in medical experiments.

247
00:19:01,521 --> 00:19:05,860
These lives in the view of Nazi ideology were not worth living.

248
00:19:05,861 --> 00:19:11,760
They began to experiment on people, and they violated the Hippocratic Oath.

249
00:19:11,860 --> 00:19:15,160
They violated any kind of norm in medical science.

250
00:19:16,280 --> 00:19:22,240
And it's not surprising that that's what people associate with Nazi Germany.

251
00:19:22,620 --> 00:19:26,060
But the story of German technology during the Hitler years

252
00:19:26,061 --> 00:19:32,040
was more complicated than the common perception of evil scientists carrying out heinous experiments.

253
00:19:32,041 --> 00:19:42,479
Well, we tend to regard science in the Nazi period purely in terms of black and white as cruel experiments done by Mingdeley or other people on these concentration

254
00:19:42,480 --> 00:19:43,440
camp prisoners.

255
00:19:43,700 --> 00:19:57,357
When in fact you have a massive scientific state that's developed that is pioneering medical techniques, that is pioneering rocketry and optical techniques, techniques

256
00:19:57,358 --> 00:20:01,200
in industry, chemistry, all aspects of science.

257
00:20:01,201 --> 00:20:10,960
So the period is much more complex and more rich and more interesting historically than the simple story of abuse of science in the concentration camps.

258
00:20:12,380 --> 00:20:16,520
Hitler's fascist regime had come to power in Germany in 1933.

259
00:20:17,640 --> 00:20:22,680
The shocking reality was it happened in one of the most scientifically advanced countries in the world.

260
00:20:23,600 --> 00:20:30,760
At the time, Germany could boast half the world's Nobel Prize winners and the best part of the world's technological patents.

261
00:20:31,200 --> 00:20:35,800
Most Jewish scientists were removed from their posts.

262
00:20:35,801 --> 00:20:39,700
A substantial minority of the rest, more or less willingly,

263
00:20:39,701 --> 00:20:41,700
became members of the Nazi party.

264
00:20:41,701 --> 00:20:46,400
Many of the scientists who refused to join the party

265
00:20:46,401 --> 00:20:50,200
continued their research and were responsible for some of the most

266
00:20:50,201 --> 00:20:52,600
important inventions of the 20th century.

267
00:20:52,601 --> 00:20:56,700
But much of this research

268
00:20:56,701 --> 00:21:00,700
had its roots in democratic Germany before Hitler even came to power.

269
00:21:00,701 --> 00:21:08,340
There were a lot of technological discoveries in many different areas from quantum physics to biology.

270
00:21:09,020 --> 00:21:13,440
So there was a real flowering of science during the Weimar period.

271
00:21:14,380 --> 00:21:18,280
One of these inventions would revolutionize scientific research.

272
00:21:19,320 --> 00:21:25,380
Work on the electron microscope had begun in 1920s Germany and came to fruition in 1931.

273
00:21:26,260 --> 00:21:28,100
Science would never be the same again.

274
00:21:28,101 --> 00:21:32,000
You cannot think of modern science without computers.

275
00:21:32,600 --> 00:21:35,500
You can also not think of modern science without the electron microscope,

276
00:21:35,620 --> 00:21:38,920
or certainly all the knowledge that has been collected with the electron microscope.

277
00:21:39,500 --> 00:21:43,560
The genius behind the discovery was Ernst Ruska,

278
00:21:44,360 --> 00:21:49,000
a young German electrical engineer based at Berlin's Technical College.

279
00:21:50,140 --> 00:21:53,600
His secretary, Lotted Lambert, worked closely with him to the end.

280
00:21:53,601 --> 00:21:56,860
Everybody liked to work with him and for him.

281
00:21:56,861 --> 00:21:58,431
He was very popular.

282
00:21:58,432 --> 00:22:04,320
He was regarded, everybody said he's the father of the electron microscope.

283
00:22:05,360 --> 00:22:10,200
His work was his life. He was what we would call today a workaholic.

284
00:22:13,980 --> 00:22:19,480
The dreadful irony was that as Germany was becoming the most murderous regime in history,

285
00:22:20,280 --> 00:22:24,300
Ruska had developed a machine that would uncover the secrets of life itself.

286
00:22:24,301 --> 00:22:29,540
The microscope had changed little in the years since it was discovered.

287
00:22:29,541 --> 00:22:33,240
But in the 20th century, it was no longer adequate.

288
00:22:33,241 --> 00:22:39,540
The power of an optical microscope is limited by the wavelengths of the light beams used to view a specimen.

289
00:22:39,541 --> 00:22:44,460
Since electrons have shorter wavelengths, they could magnify more.

290
00:22:45,820 --> 00:22:52,800
Ruska's genius was the discovery that a magnetic coil could act as a lens to focus an electron beam.

291
00:22:52,801 --> 00:22:59,840
Here on this PowerPoint presentation is a picture of the design of the first microscope that had more than one lens.

292
00:23:00,420 --> 00:23:04,760
And this design has as date 9th of March 1931.

293
00:23:05,700 --> 00:23:11,180
And here you see these areas that are crossed through here and here.

294
00:23:11,520 --> 00:23:13,400
Those are actual magnetic lenses.

295
00:23:14,040 --> 00:23:21,200
And his main invention, or one of the principal inventions, was to concentrate the magnetic field into a very narrow gap.

296
00:23:21,201 --> 00:23:28,040
And in that very narrow gap, one then has a very strong magnetic field and thus very strong lenses.

297
00:23:28,760 --> 00:23:36,080
And that is what made the high magnifications possible that he first achieved.

298
00:23:37,640 --> 00:23:41,360
This is what Ruska's electron microscope has become today.

299
00:23:41,900 --> 00:23:47,800
A revolutionary and complex piece of engineering that can investigate the actual building blocks of life.

300
00:23:47,801 --> 00:23:52,800
The electron microscope was called das Über-microskop,

301
00:23:52,801 --> 00:23:57,800
and the supermicroscope, and it was a quantum jump.

302
00:23:57,801 --> 00:24:01,800
Suddenly you can look inside cells, you can see mitochondria,

303
00:24:01,801 --> 00:24:05,800
you can see all kinds of little compartments in cells,

304
00:24:05,801 --> 00:24:10,800
and the whole organization of life within the cell was largely explored

305
00:24:10,801 --> 00:24:13,800
by the advent of the electron microscope.

306
00:24:13,801 --> 00:24:20,800
The Nazis, though, took no interest in Ruska's extraordinary discovery.

307
00:24:20,801 --> 00:24:23,800
There was, after all, no clear way in which the electron microscope

308
00:24:23,801 --> 00:24:30,800
could be used to promote Nazi militarism or propaganda or its racial policies.

309
00:24:30,801 --> 00:24:36,800
It was left to Ruska and his mentor, Max Knoll, to look elsewhere for funding and support.

310
00:24:36,801 --> 00:24:40,800
They could see the commercial potential of their invention.

311
00:24:40,801 --> 00:24:46,600
In 1930s Germany, though, they struggled to find an entrepreneur willing to finance their work.

312
00:24:46,601 --> 00:24:51,600
They trudged from pillar to post. They gave lectures.

313
00:24:51,601 --> 00:25:00,920
They tried to convince people in industry and in big firms who would take the risk to do that.

314
00:25:01,520 --> 00:25:08,100
It was difficult to convince them. They didn't believe that it would be useful.

315
00:25:08,101 --> 00:25:17,400
In Nazi Germany, Ruska, a social liberal, waited six years before the industrial giant Siemens finally decided to fund his research.

316
00:25:17,401 --> 00:25:21,880
He worked in industry, which is often seen as inner emigration.

317
00:25:22,440 --> 00:25:30,560
You weren't working at the university, teaching students, maybe having to inject ideology into your lectures.

318
00:25:31,740 --> 00:25:35,560
So the question is, would it have been better if he emigrated?

319
00:25:35,561 --> 00:25:40,180
and wood science weakened even more if all these fine scientists had left.

320
00:25:41,340 --> 00:25:46,660
Despite the difficulties, Ruska stayed on in Germany to pursue his work on the electron microscope.

321
00:25:48,200 --> 00:25:52,200
Numerous Nobel Prize winners would owe their own discoveries to Ruska's invention,

322
00:25:52,820 --> 00:25:57,100
but it was not until 1986, just two years before his death,

323
00:25:57,540 --> 00:26:00,500
that Ruska himself gained the ultimate prize for his invention.

324
00:26:00,501 --> 00:26:03,920
This was the crown of his life, the Nobel Prize.

325
00:26:03,921 --> 00:26:07,920
He said it was a recognition for electron microscopy.

326
00:26:08,880 --> 00:26:10,860
He didn't think of him personally.

327
00:26:11,100 --> 00:26:15,980
He said, finally they recognized the whole field of electron microscopy.

328
00:26:16,240 --> 00:26:18,740
How important it was. He was happy for that.

329
00:26:20,820 --> 00:26:26,120
The electron microscope may have flourished despite, rather than because of the Nazis.

330
00:26:27,000 --> 00:26:31,460
Yet in the new possibilities it opened up for research into viruses and other diseases,

331
00:26:31,461 --> 00:26:35,120
It paralleled one important aspect of Nazi ideology.

332
00:26:35,121 --> 00:26:43,260
They would call Adolf Hitler the great doctor of the German people.

333
00:26:43,261 --> 00:26:49,020
They talked about enemies of the state being like a cancer, or the Jews being like a cancer.

334
00:26:49,640 --> 00:26:53,180
They talked about a bacillus enemy people.

335
00:26:54,260 --> 00:26:58,700
And so, both I think at the metaphorical level, the policy level,

336
00:26:58,701 --> 00:27:03,380
but also at this level of just increasing the health of the German citizen

337
00:27:03,381 --> 00:27:06,540
in order to increase the health of the German state.

338
00:27:06,720 --> 00:27:11,000
Medicine and public health was wrapped all of that together.

339
00:27:12,880 --> 00:27:15,980
This was all part of Hitler's propaganda war.

340
00:27:16,920 --> 00:27:19,800
But as the 1930s gave way to the 1940s,

341
00:27:20,400 --> 00:27:24,180
Hitler had to concentrate on the very real war he was fighting against the Allies.

342
00:27:24,181 --> 00:27:29,180
Hitler now needed to acquire new military hardware.

343
00:27:29,181 --> 00:27:34,180
To achieve that, German technological know-how would play a vital part.

344
00:27:35,180 --> 00:27:40,180
The first electron microscope could magnify an object up to 17 times.

345
00:27:40,181 --> 00:27:43,180
Today's models can magnify up to 2 million times.

346
00:27:43,181 --> 00:27:46,180
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

347
00:27:47,180 --> 00:27:51,180
We now return to High Tech Hitler on Modern Marvels.

348
00:27:51,181 --> 00:27:56,180
On September the 1st, 1939, the Nazi army smashed into Poland.

349
00:27:56,181 --> 00:28:03,180
The outbreak of war in 1939 radically changed the focus of German science and technology.

350
00:28:03,181 --> 00:28:09,180
The priority now was the development of new weapons that could give Germany the military edge.

351
00:28:09,181 --> 00:28:15,980
edge. Wars tend to have a tremendous forcing function on technology.

352
00:28:15,981 --> 00:28:18,280
They tend to accelerate

353
00:28:18,281 --> 00:28:25,140
technology simply because a nation state has no higher obligation than to survive in war.

354
00:28:27,660 --> 00:28:32,860
What the war does is it shifts priorities in the direction of pragmatism,

355
00:28:32,861 --> 00:28:37,080
the pragmatism of short-term military goals.

356
00:28:39,180 --> 00:28:46,600
Almost everybody that wanted to try to do something was able to get money from the German government.

357
00:28:46,601 --> 00:28:55,780
If it was, let's say, guised in the context of a wonder weapon or a super breakthrough technology or something that could turn the tide of the war.

358
00:28:58,260 --> 00:29:05,220
Famously, the V1 and V2 flying bombs eventually became the super weapon that Hitler thought might win him the war.

359
00:29:05,221 --> 00:29:14,920
But in the early 1940s, both the Allies and Germany sought air superiority, and the key to this was ever faster planes.

360
00:29:16,360 --> 00:29:23,220
Both sides were trying to develop a fighter plane powered by a jet engine, but German engineers got there first.

361
00:29:25,000 --> 00:29:28,800
The man behind the new fighting machine was Ernst Heinkel.

362
00:29:29,480 --> 00:29:32,800
Ernst Heinkel was an individual who was really obsessed with speed.

363
00:29:32,801 --> 00:29:37,900
It seems to have been the great unifying driver in his technical life.

364
00:29:39,160 --> 00:29:44,220
Piston-driven planes were already flying in excess of 450 miles per hour,

365
00:29:45,100 --> 00:29:47,500
virtually at the limit of propeller-powered flight.

366
00:29:48,520 --> 00:29:53,000
But Heinkel was determined to go one better and become the first with the jet engine.

367
00:29:53,680 --> 00:29:57,780
And he put together a team under what he called Special Project 2,

368
00:29:57,781 --> 00:30:03,240
which was kind of highly proprietary, and we would call it a compartmented program.

369
00:30:03,460 --> 00:30:07,780
They built a special part of the factory where only certain people could go in,

370
00:30:08,220 --> 00:30:13,320
and he actually assigned a team of engineers and technicians to make this come together.

371
00:30:14,180 --> 00:30:20,260
The first successful flight of Heinkel's prototype jet-powered plane, the HE-178,

372
00:30:20,700 --> 00:30:24,360
took place over a forest in northern Germany in August 1939.

373
00:30:24,361 --> 00:30:31,220
Heichel believed the speed allowed by the jet engine would be a critical weapon, against

374
00:30:31,221 --> 00:30:34,440
which enemy planes would have no defense.

375
00:30:34,441 --> 00:30:40,360
The jet engine was basically seen as a propulsion device that could enable an airplane to fly

376
00:30:40,361 --> 00:30:44,500
at very high speeds at high altitude.

377
00:30:44,501 --> 00:30:48,960
By flying at high altitude, you have much greater aerodynamic efficiency.

378
00:30:48,961 --> 00:30:53,660
In other words, the plane flies further for a given amount of fuel.

379
00:30:54,340 --> 00:30:59,360
And the jet engine could simply accomplish this in ways that a propeller aircraft could not.

380
00:31:03,360 --> 00:31:07,680
The jet, simply gobbling air, mixing it with fuel, igniting it,

381
00:31:08,240 --> 00:31:15,720
the jet engine was clearly a design element here that could give you the potential of the 500-mile-an-hour airplane or even beyond.

382
00:31:16,040 --> 00:31:18,840
It would be a high-speed engine for high-speed airplanes.

383
00:31:18,960 --> 00:31:27,180
A week after Heinkel's prototype was successfully tested, Hitler sent German troops into Poland

384
00:31:27,181 --> 00:31:30,140
and war was declared.

385
00:31:30,141 --> 00:31:34,640
On the ground, the German army swept all before it.

386
00:31:34,641 --> 00:31:39,300
In the air, the Luftwaffe was no less ruthless.

387
00:31:39,301 --> 00:31:47,720
Germany had built its military force around the concept of Blitzkrieg or lightning war.

388
00:31:47,721 --> 00:31:56,380
And Hitler was so concerned with using the airplane as a tool of bombardment, as basically

389
00:31:56,381 --> 00:32:01,020
a bomber, that he was slow to come to the jet engine.

390
00:32:01,021 --> 00:32:06,960
The jet's speed meant it could outfly any enemy plane, either as close support for bomber

391
00:32:06,961 --> 00:32:12,900
attacks or in aerial combat as a lethal weapon against Allied bombers.

392
00:32:12,901 --> 00:32:17,780
Hitler's aggressive military instincts meant he always favored the bomber over the jet fighter.

393
00:32:18,620 --> 00:32:20,100
He would soon pay the price.

394
00:32:21,520 --> 00:32:25,160
By early 1940, the German advance through Europe was at its height.

395
00:32:25,161 --> 00:32:29,380
The battle for air superiority over the English Channel became crucial.

396
00:32:29,920 --> 00:32:33,960
The Germans broke through the charge of hurricanes and spitfires that went out to meet them.

397
00:32:34,420 --> 00:32:40,520
The Battle of Britain started on July 10th and climaxed in mid-September.

398
00:32:41,340 --> 00:32:46,060
Despite early German success, the tide of the air war would then turn.

399
00:32:51,340 --> 00:32:59,480
The Battle of Britain was, in my view, the most significant battle to take place in Europe during the Second World War.

400
00:32:59,980 --> 00:33:05,180
And on September 15th, he sent the Luftwaffe into one of its greatest attacks.

401
00:33:05,181 --> 00:33:14,360
By losing that battle, Germany from that point on was clearly on a defensive against the Allies in the West.

402
00:33:14,361 --> 00:33:20,740
Britain became a huge, impregnable, rock island aircraft carrier, if you will,

403
00:33:21,420 --> 00:33:27,600
from whence the Allies could mount a second front, an aerial second front, which they did very successfully.

404
00:33:28,440 --> 00:33:32,220
By the end of October, the Spitfire had triumphed.

405
00:33:32,221 --> 00:33:37,020
The Luftwaffe had lost 1,600 planes damaged or destroyed.

406
00:33:38,880 --> 00:33:43,960
And so we see then in the German air war, from that time on,

407
00:33:44,040 --> 00:33:46,960
there is this dramatic shift from offensive weaponry,

408
00:33:47,280 --> 00:33:51,740
which infuriates Hitler because he wants to build bombers and things to attack people,

409
00:33:52,140 --> 00:33:56,480
to having to build defensive systems simply to defend the skies of the right.

410
00:33:56,481 --> 00:34:03,440
Hitler turned to his scientists and engineers and belatedly to the jet fighter.

411
00:34:04,400 --> 00:34:09,800
He now knew that the hard-pressed Luftwaffe needed faster and more effective fighter planes.

412
00:34:11,180 --> 00:34:15,260
Heinkel's prototype 178 had been followed by the HE-280,

413
00:34:15,800 --> 00:34:19,160
which had made its first successful flight in the spring of 1941.

414
00:34:20,500 --> 00:34:24,700
Quite clearly, you see in the 280 the genesis of the modern jet fighter.

415
00:34:24,701 --> 00:34:32,560
It has space for armaments, space for sufficient fuel, a rugged structure capable of undertaking the role of a combat fighter.

416
00:34:33,320 --> 00:34:36,480
But only nine HE-280s were ever built.

417
00:34:37,360 --> 00:34:41,324
The problem for Heinkel was that he had a rival, the company run by Dr.

418
00:34:41,325 --> 00:34:42,440
Willi Messerschmidt.

419
00:34:43,320 --> 00:34:46,880
They had competed throughout the war to equip the Luftwaffe.

420
00:34:47,500 --> 00:34:50,480
More often than not, it was Messerschmidt who won out.

421
00:34:50,481 --> 00:34:56,360
Messerschmitt succeeds first of all with the Messerschmitt 109, the aircraft that we will

422
00:34:56,361 --> 00:35:00,600
always associate with the Battle of Britain, for example, and the early days of the Blitzkrieg.

423
00:35:00,601 --> 00:35:05,320
And then later on in the jet aircraft business, he succeeds very much with the Messerschmitt 262.

424
00:35:06,000 --> 00:35:11,640
Messerschmitt had been developing his own jet fighter since 1939, but always seemed one step

425
00:35:11,641 --> 00:35:16,004
behind Heinkel. Then in late 1943, Messerschmitt got his break.

426
00:35:16,005 --> 00:35:18,220
Heinkel may have been first with

427
00:35:18,221 --> 00:35:23,580
the jet fighter, but Messerschmitt's 262 was superior, and Hitler approved the plane for

428
00:35:23,581 --> 00:35:24,320
mass production.

429
00:35:25,500 --> 00:35:33,060
There was an intense rivalry, but always Messerschmitt kind of came out ahead in the fighter world.

430
00:35:33,940 --> 00:35:39,696
From the very beginning, it was clear that the MAT-62 was a far better aircraft.

431
00:35:39,697 --> 00:35:40,397
Because

432
00:35:40,398 --> 00:35:44,460
of its engine installation, it was much faster than the Heichel 280 also, and it had a much

433
00:35:44,461 --> 00:35:48,859
heavier armament. It was somewhat larger, a much longer range.

434
00:35:48,860 --> 00:35:51,200
So based on all of those factors,

435
00:35:51,320 --> 00:35:53,817
the Heinkel 280 was canceled.

436
00:35:53,818 --> 00:35:59,240
Arguably, three years had been wasted since Heinkel's 280 first

437
00:35:59,241 --> 00:36:00,418
took to the air.

438
00:36:00,419 --> 00:36:05,940
But in 1944, the Nazi high command was confident that in the Messerschmitt,

439
00:36:06,260 --> 00:36:09,291
they had a plane that could dominate the skies.

440
00:36:09,292 --> 00:36:12,580
The big question was, had the best German engineers

441
00:36:12,581 --> 00:36:15,500
produced a machine that could win the war for Hitler.

442
00:36:17,260 --> 00:36:20,820
But the new obsession with speed brought its own problems.

443
00:36:21,760 --> 00:36:24,540
The old idea of jumping out of a plane with a parachute

444
00:36:24,541 --> 00:36:29,440
would no longer work in a stricken aircraft traveling at 500 miles per hour.

445
00:36:30,480 --> 00:36:34,540
To solve this, Hitler's engineers had to invent something new.

446
00:36:34,541 --> 00:36:46,980
With a top speed of approximately 540 miles per hour, the Messerschmitt 262 was 120 miles

447
00:36:46,981 --> 00:36:51,980
per hour faster than the famed P-51 Mustang used by the Allies.

