germany as the luftwaffe tried to regain control of the skies over europe using the new generation

455
00:37:42,021 --> 00:37:48,240
of faster aircraft it became clear that the increased speeds presented german pilots with

456
00:37:48,241 --> 00:37:51,840
new dangers.

457
00:37:51,841 --> 00:37:55,900
There's no question that as speeds got higher, ejection seats were necessary.

458
00:37:55,901 --> 00:38:00,420
But even before jet engines were fully implemented,

459
00:38:00,421 --> 00:38:05,120
high-speed piston engine aircraft were also being fitted with ejection seats.

460
00:38:05,121 --> 00:38:08,780
The problem was how could a pilot escape from a disabled plane

461
00:38:08,781 --> 00:38:12,800
that was traveling in excess of 500 miles per hour?

462
00:38:12,801 --> 00:38:17,960
Jumping out with a parachute strapped on the back was no longer an option.

463
00:38:17,961 --> 00:38:22,540
If the pilot has to get out of that airplane, he is going to have a very difficult time indeed.

464
00:38:22,980 --> 00:38:26,860
One imagines him jettisoning the canopy of the aircraft

465
00:38:26,861 --> 00:38:30,820
and then clambering out of the airplane and trying to throw himself over the side.

466
00:38:31,260 --> 00:38:33,860
The aerodynamic forces can pin him inside the airplane

467
00:38:33,861 --> 00:38:36,460
or they can carry him into other portions of aircraft structure.

468
00:38:37,120 --> 00:38:42,180
Now, you really do have to throw the pilot clear of the aircraft in some fashion.

469
00:38:42,181 --> 00:38:49,180
Ernst Heinkel and his team of engineers were already grappling with the problem.

470
00:38:49,181 --> 00:38:56,180
They thought if he has to get out of the airplane, we better have some sort of catapult seat to throw him out.

471
00:38:56,181 --> 00:39:00,180
And this becomes, of course, the, as we say in the United States, the ejection seat.

472
00:39:00,181 --> 00:39:08,180
In a museum near Frankfurt, Germany, are the remains of one of the first ejection seats ever to be used in battle.

473
00:39:08,181 --> 00:39:13,360
This one was fitted to Heinkel's propeller-powered 219 Nightfighter.

474
00:39:13,560 --> 00:39:18,080
First of all, you have to trigger a handle to remove the canopy.

475
00:39:18,900 --> 00:39:25,460
Thereafter, a quick release mechanism releases the compressed air into the cannon,

476
00:39:25,960 --> 00:39:30,600
drives the piston upward, and this drives the whole seat out of the cockpit.

477
00:39:30,601 --> 00:39:38,480
it. This took approximately three seconds to activate the system.

478
00:39:41,000 --> 00:39:45,587
Luftwaffe pilot Otto Fries owes his life to this new equipment.

479
00:39:45,588 --> 00:39:47,480
Toward the end of the war,

480
00:39:47,600 --> 00:39:51,192
he was shot down at 8,000 feet over German territory.

481
00:39:51,193 --> 00:39:53,360
Blinded by blood in his eyes and

482
00:39:53,361 --> 00:39:56,920
with an engine on fire, he tried to pull the plane out of a dive.

483
00:40:00,600 --> 00:40:04,840
And then I thought to myself, it can't be true.

484
00:40:05,360 --> 00:40:07,860
This cannot be the end. There has to be a solution.

485
00:40:08,400 --> 00:40:09,400
I couldn't think straight.

486
00:40:09,720 --> 00:40:13,320
All I knew was I had to get out using the ejection seat.

487
00:40:15,340 --> 00:40:19,300
Then by chance, I found my flashlight.

488
00:40:19,301 --> 00:40:23,760
And I lit up everything and found here on this side a red flap.

489
00:40:27,000 --> 00:40:30,500
And in my excitement, I immediately pressed the red button.

490
00:40:30,600 --> 00:40:37,600
and instead of leaning my head here and the feet down, I was simply shot out.

491
00:40:37,601 --> 00:40:46,600
I detached myself from the seat, then I started tumbling,

492
00:40:46,601 --> 00:40:54,600
and as I dived through the clouds again, I thought, it is now time to pull the parachute cord.

493
00:40:54,601 --> 00:41:01,494
parachute caught. And the parachute opened and I started to descend.

494
00:41:01,495 --> 00:41:03,420
Without an ejection

495
00:41:03,421 --> 00:41:06,920
seat I would have been lost.

496
00:41:06,921 --> 00:41:12,600
In total, it is thought that the ejection seat saved the lives of over 60 German pilots

497
00:41:12,601 --> 00:41:19,040
in the last years of the war, vital at a time when experienced pilots were in short supply.

498
00:41:19,041 --> 00:41:23,320
But the technology that produced the jet engine and the ejection seat, which might have swung

499
00:41:23,321 --> 00:41:27,813
the war in Hitler's favor came too late to save the Third Reich.

500
00:41:27,814 --> 00:41:30,200
For all its technical superiority,

501
00:41:30,201 --> 00:41:35,720
the Luftwaffe never mastered how to use the faster but less maneuverable jet fighter effectively.

502
00:41:36,760 --> 00:41:43,480
When we take a look at Messerschmitt 262s in combat, we find that there was a much higher

503
00:41:43,481 --> 00:41:47,300
loss rate than the Germans actually should have experienced.

504
00:41:47,301 --> 00:41:49,720
They had thought about the technology,

505
00:41:49,721 --> 00:41:53,680
but they had not thought about the doctrinal operational tactics

506
00:41:53,681 --> 00:41:56,500
under which they would be using this airplane.

507
00:41:58,360 --> 00:42:01,540
More time might have helped the German high command,

508
00:42:02,080 --> 00:42:05,500
but it was the one thing Hitler did not have.

509
00:42:07,260 --> 00:42:12,660
From 1944 onwards, Germany was not a master of its airspace at all.

510
00:42:12,661 --> 00:42:24,480
And it is to try to offset that, that we see this explosion of interest in Germany in building jet airplanes, building rocket-powered airplanes.

511
00:42:25,140 --> 00:42:35,440
Out of all of that, the only aircraft program that they can achieve in the short term that makes any sense at all is the Messerschmitt 262.

512
00:42:35,800 --> 00:42:37,440
But it simply came too late.

513
00:42:37,441 --> 00:42:42,600
By 1944, when the Allies landed on the beaches of Normandy,

514
00:42:42,601 --> 00:42:48,220
it was five years since Ernst Heinkel first successfully tested his prototype jet engine.

515
00:42:49,300 --> 00:42:52,740
Despite its superior technology, Germany was now in retreat.

516
00:42:53,380 --> 00:42:55,040
Its air bases had been bombarded,

517
00:42:55,480 --> 00:42:59,300
and the Luftwaffe was forced to conceal its new fighter aircraft in forests

518
00:42:59,301 --> 00:43:01,660
and operate flights from autobahns.

519
00:43:02,260 --> 00:43:05,700
They produced over 2,000 Me 262s,

520
00:43:05,701 --> 00:43:08,560
But they could only train a small number of pilots.

521
00:43:08,561 --> 00:43:14,120
So even with the best aircraft, highly trained pilots make a vast difference.

522
00:43:14,300 --> 00:43:17,480
They're almost as important as the actual aircraft they're flying.

523
00:43:18,380 --> 00:43:23,000
We take a look at the history of aerodynamics in the 20th century and high-speed flight.

524
00:43:23,360 --> 00:43:28,220
Never did a country squander so great a technical advantage as Germany had.

525
00:43:29,420 --> 00:43:33,120
Had Hitler trusted the new technology and opted sooner for the jet fighter,

526
00:43:33,121 --> 00:43:36,640
the course of the war, if not its outcome, might have been different.

527
00:43:37,660 --> 00:43:41,280
That was the problem with the Nazi attitude toward technological research.

528
00:43:42,020 --> 00:43:46,340
Some science thrived, especially if it advanced the Nazis' ideological aims.

529
00:43:46,800 --> 00:43:49,960
But much of it was delayed, or in some cases, dismissed.

530
00:43:50,900 --> 00:43:57,160
Scientists, physicians, their professions were banned to them unless they supported the Nazi ideology.

531
00:43:57,161 --> 00:44:03,140
We're more familiar with the cases where the Nazis actually suppressed science.

532
00:44:03,980 --> 00:44:07,940
They launched an attack on what was called Deutsche Physik, or German physics,

533
00:44:08,660 --> 00:44:13,440
and supported what they called Aryan physics against Jewish physics.

534
00:44:14,100 --> 00:44:16,700
On the other hand, other aspects of science were actually promoted.

535
00:44:16,701 --> 00:44:22,560
So it divides according to whether the Nazi regime thought this was a threat to Germany

536
00:44:22,561 --> 00:44:27,080
or was something that could be advanced to help the German race.

537
00:44:27,160 --> 00:44:34,440
Although the legacy of German technology is all around us, it was this narrow and distorted

538
00:44:34,441 --> 00:44:38,560
view that prevailed during the Hitler years.

539
00:44:38,561 --> 00:44:43,560
It meant that much of the genius of German science at the time went unrecognized by the

540
00:44:43,561 --> 00:44:49,340
Nazis, thriving despite rather than because of fascist ideology.

